A||||12||mshill||16" rims and tires...||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||08-08-2000||05:21 PM||||Ok, I have read about 50 old posts about tires and rims and am now more confused than ever.<P>I want 16" rims!!!!<P>If I go with a 16x7 35mm offset, what is the widest tire I can put on the back without coil-overs?<P>If I go with a 16x8 rim for he rears: What offset do I need?  What is the largest tire I can use without coil-overs?  How about with coil-overs?<P>This is on an 86GT.||||reg||5
Z||000001||loafer87gt||08-08-2000||07:15 PM||||I remember reading on the Fiero digest that the widest tires you can fit on a 7" rim and still have OK clearance on the back is a 255 width tire.||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||08-08-2000||07:52 PM||||don't put that much tire on that narrow a wheel!... thats crappy handeling and highspeed sway just waiting to unleash. on a 7" wide wheel a 225 or maybe a 235. if you go any larger than that you'll have too much tire unsupported. on and 8" wide wheel no larger than 255 should be put on it.. <A HREF="http://www.yokohamatire.com" TARGET=_blank>www.yokohamatire.com</A>  they will tell you how large a tire to put on what size wheel and still be happy with the ride of the car. i'd look at 38mm offset for a 16x7 and 42mm for 8". My 17x8s were 45mm and had 255s with no scrubbing. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||08-09-2000||12:22 AM||||1FST2M6,  Thanks.  That is the info I needed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||08-09-2000||03:07 AM||||bf goodrich and summit are what i went by. both recommend a 7" wide rim as the minimum for a 255/50-16 tire. i have 7.5" x 16 rims with that size on back. if i could get the style of wheel i have at 8.5" i'd like it better, but i'm satisfied with the looks and performance of this set up. while i haven't switched to coilovers yet i will need to.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||08:34 AM||||the method to my madness... all the tire manufactures recommend for the best wear,  performance and saftey to not have more than 1" of unsupported tire on a given side of a wheel. 7" wide wheel 255-60-14 (255 section width) 255mm/25.4=10.04"  10.04"-7"(wheel width)=3.04"(unsupported tire) 3.04'/2(# of sides the tire will have eg. outside and fender side)= 1.52" on both sides of the tire unsupported... which would be fine if your drag racing and not turning. the unsupported area would force the tire to wrinkle like a real wrinkle wall Mickey Thompson E/T. but on the street it just causes the slip angle to increase. meaning you toss your car in to a hard corner or evasive manuver to not hit a brick in the road and your car, quick to react, will handle normaly while the tire is in its shift until you fold the tire on the the unsupported part where it will break loose and send you in to a tail spin. Thats how it was explained to me at the Subaru/Panoz Driving school i took, you can read basicly the same thing on their site and at yokohamas site. with too much unsupported tire you'll have strange wear patterns and you'll have to run more psi in the tire to keep it from folding in on itself and wearing the out side of the tire in stead of the whole thing. Where if you put a 165 on a 7" wide wheel you'd have the center of the tire wearing incorrectly. the wider tire i'm sure looks better but wider isn't always better. with 195-60-14s (A032s)and a steady driver my SE would pull over 1g on a 200' skid pad at Road Atlanta. and would pull to 1g on the 205s (A509)  Not trying to start anything just explaining myself. So you can have the math too. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||08-09-2000||11:15 AM||||1FST2M6/now-a-GT--At the risk of asking a very basic question, can you tell me exactly what the "offset" is?  I understood it to be the distance between the mounting face of the hub and the outside edge of the inside of the wheel (almost confused myself on that one).  If so, then the greater the offset, the more wheel you'd have in toward the suspension.  I tried a set of wheels off of a Beretta GT (didn't measure the offset, but it looked fairly close to ours) and it rubbed the bottom of the suspension on my '86.  Straighten me out, please.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||11:49 AM||||From the Subaru/Panoz web page... <P>Wheel width plays a critical role in just how big of a tire can be stuffed under the fender. Wheel width is the width measured from each inside face of the rim - not from outside face to outside face. Which brings us to the next topic ñ wheel offset, and it is perhaps the most confusing and difficult to understand. It is the distance from the mounting face of the wheel to the centerline of the wheel. <P>Offset and backspacing are just two different ways of talking about the same thing ... how far the wheel mounting face is from the inside edge of the wheel. European and Japanese manufacturers usually talk about offset, and because of that, it's usually measured in millimeters. Backspacing is the way most U.S. companies refer to it ñ and you guessed it, they usually talk about it in inches. Either way, it's the same thing, with one small difference: Backspacing is a lot easier to measure. <P><BR>offset = (the width, including rim, divided by 2) minus backspacing <P>To get from offset to backspacing, use this formula: <P>backspacing = (the width, including rim, divided by 2) minus offset <P>Remember that everything in either formula has to be in either inches or millimeters to get the right result. <P>Most factory and European aftermarket wheels have the offset cast right on the wheel. Look in the hub area ñ you'll see the wheel size (example: 15Jx7) and a number (such as ET38). The "15" is the wheel diameter, the "J" describes the rim design, and the "x7" tells us it's a 7" wide wheel. "ET38" is the offset in mm. <P>A higher offset moves the wheel toward the center of the car, while a lower offset moves the wheel out. A 35mm offset might rub the fender, while a 38mm offset would clear it,  <P>that make more sense? i'll keep looking for more info for ya.. i'm not sure why the other wheels scrubbed.. there is a guy close to me that has 16" Baretta GTZ wheels that he's had for years on his car.. <BR>my 17s were (17x7.5)42mm up front and (17x8) 45mm out back. an 18s will be 45mm all the way around.  hope that helps a bit.. if not i'll check back and try and square it away. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||08-09-2000||01:19 PM||||The concept of Offset is basically useless in my opinion.<BR>     Here's why: when concerned with suspension geometry/ wheel bearing loads, the critical number is the ratio of front spacing to back spacing.<BR>     When concerned with body/suspension clearance, the important figures are front spacing and back spacing.<BR>     For instance: the stock Fiero wheels have ~3" of front spacing (I think). To look right on a Fiero, another wheel should have 3 to 3 1/4 inches of front spacing. 17" wheels on the front of a Fiero use up to 5 to 5 1/2 inches of backspacing to clear the suspension. 3 1/4 + 5 1/2 = 8 3/4 width, but the offset would be 29 mm.<BR>     However, these fictional wheels would IMPROVE suspension geometry by reducing the scrub radius, thereby bettering road feel and feedback and reducing steering effort.<BR>     Also, tire height comes into play. If you use taller tires than stock scrub radius will be decreased because of kingpin inclination. I went from 205/60-15 to 235/60-15 tires (on stck wheels) on the front of my '86 SE and steering effort was reduced noticeably. If I went to 17 x 8 with 3" front spacing (and used the same make/model of tire) steering effort would be further reduced.||||reg||4
Z||000009||Monkeyman||08-09-2000||01:36 PM||||Hell.  This is getting too confusing for me.  All I need to know is...what is the widest, lowest profile tire I can run on my '85 GT with the stock suspension (no coil overs) on 17" rims?  I don't want the roller skate/homeboy/ricer look.  I want decent handling and looks.  Money is a factor.  (I know I can do it cheaper with 16" rims, but I like the looks of a really low profile tire.)  I understand I'll lose some of the ride quality with lower profile tire, but that's OK.  I also want to make sure my speedo stays fairly accurate.  I don't plan on autoxing, but I still want to corner with the big boys.<P>I guess all I need is wheel sizes (F/R) and tire sizes (F/R).  1FST2M4--Didn't you have coil overs??||||reg||1
Z||000010||1FST2M6||08-09-2000||02:24 PM||||nope no coil overs.. Konis and Eibachs.. 17x7.5 42mm offset up front 215-40-17 (looks better than 45 series and easy to come by too) Dunlop W10s or Yokohama A520s are great tires and at a great price... or the Kumho ECSTA. as for the rear 17x8 45mm offset 245-40-17 cheaper than a 255 and easyier to find if your tire get eaten by a strom door in the highway at 55+. or 17x7 42mm all the way around with a 215-40.. your speed o will be off about .6mph @ 60.  check <A HREF="http://www.washingtontire.com" TARGET=_blank>www.washingtontire.com</A>  for wheels... and <A HREF="http://www.discounttiredirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.discounttiredirect.com</A>  for tires.<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||08-10-2000||02:04 AM||||Thanx, 1FSTGT.  That will do just fine.  I can't afford them now (probably an early spring thing), but at least I know what to look for in case I find something on sale.||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||08-10-2000||08:53 AM||||sure anytime! ||||reg||1
